<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hasdrubal the Fair</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 270–<a href="/years/221-bce">221 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcid-family">Barcid family</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hasdrubal the Fair was a Carthaginian general, son-in-law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamilcar-barca">Hamilcar Barca</a>, who succeeded him as commander in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> in about 229 BCE. He extended Carthaginian power by diplomacy, founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">Qart Hadasht</a> (Carthago Nova) around 227 BCE, and in 226 BCE agreed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to treat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> as a limit. He was assassinated in 221 BCE and succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hasdrubal-the-fair.jpg" alt="Hasdrubal the Fair" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maarten Dirkse · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AsdrubalBusto.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hamilcar Barca, then <b>Hasdrubal the Fair</b>, founder of Qart Hadasht (Cartagena), and from 221 BCE Hannibal conquered the Iberians south of the Ebro, and Hannibal’s siege of Saguntum, an ally of Rome, began the Second Punic War.</blockquote><a href="/culture/iberians">Iberians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>After his death in about 229 BCE, his son-in-law <b>Hasdrubal</b> extended Carthaginian power by diplomacy and founded Qart Hadasht, later Carthago Nova, around 227 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/barcid-iberia">Barcid Iberia</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 227 BCE the Carthaginian general <b>Hasdrubal</b> founded Qart Hadasht, later Carthago Nova, probably on or near the site of Mastia, making it the capital of Barcid Iberia.</blockquote><a href="/state/mastieni">Mastieni</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 220s BCE it made an alliance with Rome, though it lay south of the Ebro, the limit agreed between Rome and <b>Hasdrubal</b> in 226 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/saguntum">Saguntum</a></li></ul></section>
